00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:53,040
It mainly covers some basic concepts of assembly language.

58
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:58,190
Let's first take a look at exactly what it is.

59
00:02:58,190 --> 00:02:59,570
What is assembly language?

60
00:02:59,570 --> 00:03:03,090
Back in our first course,

61
00:03:03,090 --> 00:03:04,650
When we talked about the history of language evolution,

62
00:03:04,650 --> 00:03:06,810
I actually already touched on this briefly,

63
00:03:06,810 --> 00:03:07,030
right?

64
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:08,470
You all know that assembly language

65
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:14,150
is basically machine language that's been slightly modified,

66
00:03:14,150 --> 00:03:15,410
turned into a way of writing

67
00:03:15,410 --> 00:03:18,550
that we humans can understand reasonably well,

68
00:03:18,550 --> 00:03:18,710
right?

69
00:03:18,710 --> 00:03:20,070
So it's a kind of...

70
00:03:20,070 --> 00:03:21,630
assembly language,

71
00:03:21,630 --> 00:03:22,690
in English,

72
00:03:22,690 --> 00:03:24,750
is called Assembly Language,

73
00:03:24,750 --> 00:03:27,870
and it's a low-level language.

74
00:03:27,870 --> 00:03:29,110
Now, note this:

75
00:03:29,110 --> 00:03:30,630
when we say "low-level",

76
00:03:30,630 --> 00:03:34,930
it means that compared to our high-level languages,

77
00:03:34,930 --> 00:03:40,190
its level of abstraction is relatively lower.

78
00:03:40,190 --> 00:03:42,370
As for what we mean by abstraction level,

79
00:03:42,370 --> 00:03:44,650
you've probably learned some high-level languages,

80
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:47,250
like C, or things like C++ or Java.

81
00:03:47,250 --> 00:03:49,830
So where do you think this so-called 'high-level' aspect comes in?

82
00:03:49,830 --> 00:03:53,330
It introduces concepts that are closer to

83
00:03:53,330 --> 00:03:55,330
things we humans can understand fairly well, right?

84
00:03:55,330 --> 00:03:57,430
For example, some object-oriented concepts,

85
00:03:57,430 --> 00:03:59,810
structs,

86
00:03:59,810 --> 00:04:01,810
and things like procedures and functions.

87
00:04:01,810 --> 00:04:04,610
Now, this assembly language,

88
00:04:04,610 --> 00:04:05,590
what we call 'low-level',

89
00:04:05,590 --> 00:04:07,430
is actually low-level in the sense that

90
00:04:07,430 --> 00:04:09,230
it is closer to

91
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:14,410
the machine language of the computer's processor.

92
00:04:14,410 --> 00:04:16,710
When we program in it,

93
00:04:16,710 --> 00:04:18,070
we actually need to understand

94
00:04:18,070 --> 00:04:20,810
more, and understand more deeply, some things about the hardware.

95
00:04:20,810 --> 00:04:23,330
Only then will it be easier to use well.

96
00:04:23,330 --> 00:04:26,190
It's not like when you've only learned a high-level language,

97
00:04:26,190 --> 00:04:31,950
where you actually end up forgetting some things about how the computer really works.

98
00:04:31,950 --> 00:04:34,390
So this,

99
00:04:34,390 --> 00:04:37,450
when we talk about this assembly language,

100
00:04:37,450 --> 00:04:41,630
it has some of these drawbacks, but it also has its advantages.

101
00:04:41,630 --> 00:04:42,430
So, here,

102
00:04:42,430 --> 00:04:43,950
let me summarize this for you again.

103
00:04:43,950 --> 00:04:45,070
First,

104
00:04:45,070 --> 00:04:48,670
let's take a look at the drawbacks of assembly language.

105
00:04:48,670 --> 00:04:50,670
Because when we talk about assembly language,

106
00:04:50,670 --> 00:04:52,330
since you haven't had much contact with it or used it much,

107
00:04:52,330 --> 00:04:55,470
you might feel a little bit afraid of it,

108
00:04:55,470 --> 00:04:55,690
right?

109
00:04:55,690 --> 00:04:56,870
And that's actually true,

110
00:04:56,870 --> 00:04:59,430
because when you program in assembly language,

111
00:04:59,430 --> 00:05:01,610
it's really not that convenient.

112
00:05:01,610 --> 00:05:02,370
As we just said,

113
00:05:02,370 --> 00:05:03,390
it's a bit on the low-level side.

114
00:05:03,390 --> 00:05:04,950
So its

115
00:05:04,950 --> 00:05:06,330
drawbacks show up in

116
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:07,030
three areas.

117
00:05:07,030 --> 00:05:08,430
First, it's hard to read.

118
00:05:08,430 --> 00:05:09,670
Second, it's hard to write.

119
00:05:09,670 --> 00:05:10,230
And third,

120
00:05:10,230 --> 00:05:11,090
it's fairly hard to port.

121
00:05:11,090 --> 00:05:11,650
Right?

122
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:13,130
Think about it for a moment.

123
00:05:13,130 --> 00:05:13,610
That is to say,

124
00:05:13,610 --> 00:05:14,830
what do we mean by 'hard to read'?

125
00:05:14,830 --> 00:05:16,030
I think all of you

126
00:05:16,030 --> 00:05:18,450
should know if you've learned some assembly language,

127
00:05:18,450 --> 00:05:19,110
that

128
00:05:19,110 --> 00:05:19,930
when it comes to writing it,

129
00:05:19,930 --> 00:05:21,470
it's pretty obscure.

130
00:05:21,470 --> 00:05:22,490
Unlike high-level languages,

131
00:05:22,490 --> 00:05:23,970
maybe some simple things,

132
00:05:23,970 --> 00:05:24,450
that is to say,

133
00:05:24,450 --> 00:05:26,050
if you want to implement the same functionality,

134
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:28,330
a high-level language might do it in one or two statements.

135
00:05:28,330 --> 00:05:29,550
But with assembly language,

136
00:05:29,550 --> 00:05:31,550
you have to write many statements,

137
00:05:31,550 --> 00:05:32,910
because it's basically one statement

138
00:05:32,910 --> 00:05:34,370
corresponding to one machine instruction.

139
00:05:34,370 --> 00:05:35,770
And on top of that,

140
00:05:35,770 --> 00:05:37,990
because it involves things like registers,

141
00:05:37,990 --> 00:05:39,330
and various other operations.

142
00:05:39,330 --> 00:05:40,910
So the concept of registers

143
00:05:40,910 --> 00:05:42,730
is very tightly coupled with the hardware.

144
00:05:42,730 --> 00:05:44,230
If you don't understand the hardware,

145
00:05:44,230 --> 00:05:47,350
it's actually quite hard to write.

146
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:50,430
So the second point is that it's hard to write.

147
00:05:50,430 --> 00:05:50,850
Right?

148
00:05:50,850 --> 00:05:52,430
Hard to read and hard to write.

149
00:05:52,430 --> 00:05:54,570
Now, as for being hard to port,

150
00:05:54,570 --> 00:05:58,830
can you figure this out on your own?

151
00:05:58,830 --> 00:06:00,710
That is, why do we say it's relatively hard to port?

152
00:06:00,710 --> 00:06:03,550
What exactly do we mean by porting?

153
00:06:03,550 --> 00:06:05,690
Have any of you run into this issue before?

154
00:06:05,690 --> 00:06:10,070
Well, the difficulty of porting is actually quite easy to understand.

155
00:06:10,070 --> 00:06:12,790
As we know, assembly language

156
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:15,610
corresponds one-to-one with machine instructions, basically.

157
00:06:15,610 --> 00:06:17,890
So with different instruction set architectures,

158
00:06:17,890 --> 00:06:20,770
their machine language is definitely different.

159
00:06:20,770 --> 00:06:21,310
Think about it, isn't that so?

160
00:06:21,310 --> 00:06:22,870
Different CPU types

161
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:23,590
Take x86, for example,

162
00:06:23,590 --> 00:06:24,930
or, say, ARM.

163
00:06:24,930 --> 00:06:26,990
Their registers,

164
00:06:26,990 --> 00:06:28,430
the numbers of them are all different,

165
00:06:28,430 --> 00:06:30,610
and their functions are all different.

166
00:06:30,610 --> 00:06:32,830
So when it comes to operating on these different registers,

167
00:06:32,830 --> 00:06:34,350
the instructions and so on are different too.

168
00:06:34,350 --> 00:06:37,090
So writing them is really two completely separate sets of things.

169
00:06:37,090 --> 00:06:38,970
Some of the concepts are kind of similar,

170
00:06:38,970 --> 00:06:40,570
but writing them is really two completely separate sets of things.

171
00:06:40,570 --> 00:06:41,850
Now, given that this is the case,

172
00:06:41,850 --> 00:06:43,070
just imagine this.

173
00:06:43,070 --> 00:06:44,850
That is, suppose we want to implement the same functionality.

174
00:06:44,850 --> 00:06:48,790
After I've written a set of assembly instructions for an x86 machine,

175
00:06:48,790 --> 00:06:50,770
if I take those instructions and run them on,

176
00:06:50,770 --> 00:06:54,330
say, an ARM machine,

177
00:06:54,330 --> 00:06:56,110
they're definitely not going to run.

178
00:06:56,110 --> 00:06:57,290
In fact, you'd have to rewrite them.

179
00:06:57,290 --> 00:06:59,070
So what this shows is that,

180
00:06:59,070 --> 00:07:00,210
it's quite hard to port.

181
00:07:00,210 --> 00:07:00,990
Unlike high-level source code,

182
00:07:00,990 --> 00:07:01,750
with a high-level language,

183
00:07:01,750 --> 00:07:03,350
you really only write the code once,

184
00:07:03,350 --> 00:07:05,370
and you don't have to care at all

185
00:07:05,370 --> 00:07:07,790
about these differences at the CPU level underneath,

186
00:07:07,790 --> 00:07:08,290
right?

187
00:07:08,290 --> 00:07:11,790
When you say you write it once,

188
00:07:11,790 --> 00:07:13,610
the final translation

189
00:07:13,610 --> 00:07:14,630
is actually done by the compiler,

190
00:07:14,630 --> 00:07:16,670
which shields you from that whole layer.

191
00:07:16,670 --> 00:07:17,770
Think about it — isn't that the case?

192
00:07:17,770 --> 00:07:20,430
Now, assembly language,

193
00:07:20,430 --> 00:07:23,390
while its drawbacks are pretty obvious,

194
00:07:23,390 --> 00:07:25,490
its advantages are also very clear.

195
00:07:25,490 --> 00:07:25,810
Right?

196
00:07:25,810 --> 00:07:28,450
Since you're dealing directly with the hardware,

197
00:07:28,450 --> 00:07:31,150
its advantage shows up in the fact that

198
00:07:31,150 --> 00:07:32,910
it is extremely flexible and powerful.

199
00:07:32,910 --> 00:07:34,390
By flexible, I mean

200
00:07:34,390 --> 00:07:35,830
you can imagine this:

201
00:07:35,830 --> 00:07:38,270
也就是说, when we usually use a high-level language

202
00:07:38,270 --> 00:07:39,670
to write these programs,

203
00:07:39,670 --> 00:07:42,250
in the end, it has to go through the compiler,

204
00:07:42,250 --> 00:07:43,870
which translates it into machine instructions for you,

205
00:07:43,870 --> 00:07:46,490
before it can run on the CPU.

206
00:07:46,490 --> 00:07:46,770
Right?

207
00:07:46,770 --> 00:07:49,890
So, since the compiler is the one doing the translation for you,

208
00:07:49,890 --> 00:07:52,970
the optimization process in between,

209
00:07:52,970 --> 00:07:55,390
and how the machine instructions are organized,

210
00:07:55,390 --> 00:07:57,750
is actually handled by the compiler for you.

211
00:07:57,750 --> 00:08:00,210
You have no way to step in and intervene in any of this.

212
00:08:00,210 --> 00:08:03,590
But if you want to

213
00:08:03,590 --> 00:08:06,890
optimize these machine instructions more deeply,

214
00:08:06,890 --> 00:08:09,670
and bring out the machine's characteristics,

215
00:08:09,670 --> 00:08:11,030
its capabilities,

216
00:08:11,030 --> 00:08:12,490
and fully exploit them,

217
00:08:12,490 --> 00:08:14,430
then you actually still have to

218
00:08:14,430 --> 00:08:16,510
go down to the lowest level of machine instructions,

219
00:08:16,510 --> 00:08:17,610
at that level,

220
00:08:17,610 --> 00:08:18,910
and optimize at that level.

221
00:08:18,910 --> 00:08:19,870
So at this point,

222
00:08:19,870 --> 00:08:22,290
you can flexibly organize things on your own,

223
00:08:22,290 --> 00:08:24,850
these kinds of operations on the hardware.

224
00:08:24,850 --> 00:08:26,730
That is to say, you can bypass the compiler.

225
00:08:26,730 --> 00:08:28,150
Put simply, you bypass the compiler

226
00:08:28,150 --> 00:08:28,970
to do these things.

227
00:08:28,970 --> 00:08:31,450
So this shows a principle:

228
00:08:31,450 --> 00:08:33,410
the lower-level a feature is,

229
00:08:33,410 --> 00:08:34,430
the more flexible it is.

230
00:08:34,430 --> 00:08:35,070
Right?

231
00:08:35,070 --> 00:08:37,630
In fact, if you've used C,

232
00:08:37,630 --> 00:08:38,530
and then you use Java,

233
00:08:38,530 --> 00:08:39,530
or some other high-level language,

234
00:08:39,530 --> 00:08:40,090
like Python,

235
00:08:40,090 --> 00:08:41,310
you can feel this too.

236
00:08:41,310 --> 00:08:41,610
Right?

237
00:08:41,610 --> 00:08:42,810
Because C, compared to

238
00:08:42,810 --> 00:08:44,410
other high-level languages,

239
00:08:44,410 --> 00:08:45,810
is also a bit on the low-level side.

240
00:08:45,810 --> 00:08:46,970
Because many high-level languages...

241
00:08:46,970 --> 00:08:48,630
Actually, underneath it is written in C,

242
00:08:48,630 --> 00:08:50,110
to write some of its libraries.

243
00:08:50,110 --> 00:08:52,230
So once you have mastered C,

244
00:08:52,230 --> 00:08:53,270
you can actually write some

245
00:08:53,270 --> 00:08:55,370
more efficient programs,

246
00:08:55,370 --> 00:08:56,650
and they'll run faster too.

247
00:08:56,650 --> 00:09:00,150
Now, with this flexibility,

248
00:09:00,150 --> 00:09:01,210
what makes it so powerful is that

249
00:09:01,210 --> 00:09:02,250
we can operate at the low level

250
00:09:02,250 --> 00:09:04,550
and control the hardware directly,

251
00:09:04,550 --> 00:09:06,550
doing deeper optimizations.

252
00:09:06,550 --> 00:09:09,190
This is where your ability really shows.

253
00:09:09,190 --> 00:09:11,310
So that's why these things are

254
00:09:11,310 --> 00:09:13,770
after you've mastered assembly language,

255
00:09:13,770 --> 00:09:14,670
there are certain things that

256
00:09:14,670 --> 00:09:15,810
in high-level languages

257
00:09:15,810 --> 00:09:16,850
simply cannot be done at all,

258
00:09:16,850 --> 00:09:17,450
but assembly can do them all.

259
00:09:17,450 --> 00:09:20,530
Now some students may ask,

260
00:09:20,530 --> 00:09:22,250
so should I actually use assembly?

261
00:09:22,250 --> 00:09:24,230
What kinds of things does it do?

262
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:25,550
Since it is so flexible and powerful,

263
00:09:25,550 --> 00:09:26,530
then we say

264
00:09:26,530 --> 00:09:27,510
naturally,

265
00:09:27,510 --> 00:09:28,970
as we've already mentioned repeatedly just now,

266
00:09:28,970 --> 00:09:30,030
that is, it can actually

267
00:09:30,030 --> 00:09:32,010
directly manipulate the hardware

268
00:09:32,010 --> 00:09:32,930
in a direct way.

269
00:09:32,930 --> 00:09:34,290
So the lower level you go,

270
00:09:34,290 --> 00:09:35,890
the closer you get to the hardware,

271
00:09:35,890 --> 00:09:37,370
in places where you access the hardware,

272
00:09:37,370 --> 00:09:39,030
the more we will use assembly.

273
00:09:39,030 --> 00:09:40,810
For example,

274
00:09:40,810 --> 00:09:41,770
writing drivers,

275
00:09:41,770 --> 00:09:43,510
writing the operating system's

276
00:09:43,510 --> 00:09:45,070
bootloader,

277
00:09:45,070 --> 00:09:46,330
these kinds of parts

278
00:09:46,330 --> 00:09:47,650
will all use

279
00:09:47,650 --> 00:09:48,690
this assembly language.

280
00:09:48,690 --> 00:09:49,590
But, generally speaking,

281
00:09:49,590 --> 00:09:51,410
It won't write large chunks of assembly

282
00:09:51,410 --> 00:09:53,010
because if your program

283
00:09:53,010 --> 00:09:54,670
needs a lot of assembly written for it,

284
00:09:54,670 --> 00:09:55,330
then actually,

285
00:09:55,330 --> 00:09:56,830
we'd actually recommend that you

286
00:09:56,830 --> 00:09:58,450
write it in a high-level language instead,

287
00:09:58,450 --> 00:09:59,730
because the maintainability

288
00:09:59,730 --> 00:10:00,310
will probably be better,

289
00:10:00,310 --> 00:10:01,350
and so will the readability.

290
00:10:01,350 --> 00:10:02,590
There's one more thing,

291
00:10:02,590 --> 00:10:03,030
which is,

292
00:10:03,030 --> 00:10:04,710
if you want to squeeze out

293
00:10:04,710 --> 00:10:05,730
the ultimate performance,

294
00:10:05,730 --> 00:10:07,730
then, as we just said,

295
00:10:07,730 --> 00:10:08,590
since it requires

296
00:10:08,590 --> 00:10:09,410
this kind of flexibility,

297
00:10:09,410 --> 00:10:11,170
we can actually do that.

298
00:10:11,170 --> 00:10:12,830
If your mastery of assembly language

299
00:10:12,830 --> 00:10:14,470
is at a really advanced level,

300
00:10:14,470 --> 00:10:15,610
then in practice you can

301
00:10:15,610 --> 00:10:17,070
you don't even need to rely on the compiler

302
00:10:17,070 --> 00:10:17,830
to do some optimizations

303
00:10:17,830 --> 00:10:19,870
you can optimize the instructions completely on your own

304
00:10:19,870 --> 00:10:20,810
and that's perfectly fine

305
00:10:20,810 --> 00:10:24,890
So this is what we call

306
00:10:24,890 --> 00:10:26,130
the application scenario of assembly

307
00:10:26,130 --> 00:10:28,050
That is to say, a lot of times, if

308
00:10:28,050 --> 00:10:30,650
you are interested in some low-level stuff

309
00:10:30,650 --> 00:10:31,390
if you're really into it,

310
00:10:31,390 --> 00:10:33,250
or even sometimes maybe you want to

311
00:10:33,250 --> 00:10:34,130
build one yourself,

312
00:10:34,130 --> 00:10:35,490
something kind of cool,

313
00:10:35,490 --> 00:10:37,630
like a hack, or, to put it less politely,

314
00:10:37,630 --> 00:10:38,390
if you want to be a hacker,

315
00:10:38,390 --> 00:10:40,470
then mastering assembly

316
00:10:40,470 --> 00:10:41,950
is absolutely essential,

317
00:10:41,950 --> 00:10:42,930
because a lot of

318
00:10:42,930 --> 00:10:43,810
well, for example,

319
00:10:43,810 --> 00:10:45,890
take some little viruses for instance,

320
00:10:45,890 --> 00:10:47,130
a lot of them are written in assembly

321
00:10:47,130 --> 00:10:47,450
right?

322
00:10:47,450 --> 00:10:49,590
Because a virus itself has to be quite small,

323
00:10:49,590 --> 00:10:52,210
it can be implanted very easily.

324
00:10:52,210 --> 00:10:54,410
So these are some

325
00:10:54,410 --> 00:10:56,110
basic concepts of assembly

326
00:10:56,110 --> 00:10:57,870
that everyone should get a handle on first.

327
00:10:57,870 --> 00:11:00,410
Now, after saying all this,

328
00:11:00,410 --> 00:11:02,110
when it comes to writing assembly,

329
00:11:02,110 --> 00:11:03,430
it is also a language,

330
00:11:03,430 --> 00:11:04,530
and so, what is this language

331
00:11:04,530 --> 00:11:05,610
actually like?

332
00:11:05,610 --> 00:11:07,990
Let's take a look at it together next.

333
00:11:07,990 --> 00:11:10,210
So, about this

334
00:11:10,210 --> 00:11:13,270
assembly language,

335
00:11:13,270 --> 00:11:15,370
one thing to note,

336
00:11:15,370 --> 00:11:17,710
is that when you write assembly language,

337
00:11:17,710 --> 00:11:18,630
its syntax

338
00:11:18,630 --> 00:11:21,970
actually depends, to some extent,

339
00:11:21,970 --> 00:11:23,630
on the assembler you are using.

340
00:11:23,630 --> 00:11:24,710
Different assemblers

341
00:11:24,710 --> 00:11:27,830
the syntax formats it supports are actually a bit different

342
00:11:27,830 --> 00:11:29,250
So on our side,

343
00:11:29,250 --> 00:11:31,430
let's define the scope of what we cover.

344
00:11:31,430 --> 00:11:33,210
Because when we do the exercises later,

345
00:11:33,210 --> 00:11:34,530
the toolchain we provide by default

346
00:11:34,530 --> 00:11:36,730
— that is, the compilation tools —

347
00:11:36,730 --> 00:11:39,350
actually uses this GNU toolchain,

348
00:11:39,350 --> 00:11:41,250
the compiler,

349
00:11:41,250 --> 00:11:41,990
the assembler.

350
00:11:41,990 --> 00:11:43,330
So,

351
00:11:43,330 --> 00:11:45,190
all of the material I'm introducing here

352
00:11:45,190 --> 00:11:46,570
is really limited to

353
00:11:46,570 --> 00:11:47,050
— that is,

354
00:11:47,050 --> 00:11:49,330
making sure it's syntax that will

355
00:11:49,330 --> 00:11:50,350
run on the GNU

356
00:11:50,350 --> 00:11:51,510
assembler.

357
00:11:51,510 --> 00:11:52,550
Now, some of you,

358
00:11:52,550 --> 00:11:54,030
if you take our code as it is now

359
00:11:54,030 --> 00:11:56,410
and put it into a different assembler,

360
00:11:56,410 --> 00:11:57,350
say one of the Intel ones,

361
00:11:57,350 --> 00:11:59,210
Or if it's ARM's own built-in

362
00:11:59,210 --> 00:12:00,610
assembler,

363
00:12:00,610 --> 00:12:01,690
it might not recognize it.

364
00:12:01,690 --> 00:12:02,790
Please keep that in mind.

365
00:12:02,790 --> 00:12:06,260
There is a difference.

366
00:12:06,260 --> 00:12:08,740
Now let's take a look at

367
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:10,140
how to actually write an assembly program.

368
00:12:10,140 --> 00:12:13,260
Actually, an assembly program is still relatively

369
00:12:13,260 --> 00:12:16,260
not too complicated in terms of syntax.

370
00:12:16,260 --> 00:12:18,640
In terms of the overall framework,

371
00:12:18,640 --> 00:12:19,600
let's say one,

372
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:21,460
I've written it out here for you.

373
00:12:21,460 --> 00:12:22,900
That is, an assembly program,

374
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:24,920
for example, an assembly program,

375
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:26,180
is written as a file,

376
00:12:26,180 --> 00:12:29,120
and the suffix is usually a lowercase s,

377
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:31,600
or an uppercase S.

378
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:35,500
Now, between the lowercase s and the uppercase S,

379
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:37,100
do you all understand the difference yet?

380
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:38,700
I did briefly mention it last time.

381
00:12:38,700 --> 00:12:40,020
Remember this.

382
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:41,600
Sometimes you may have noticed

383
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:42,340
that some assembly files

384
00:12:42,340 --> 00:12:43,880
have a lowercase s as the file extension,

385
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:45,240
while others use an uppercase S.

386
00:12:45,240 --> 00:12:48,880
In the exercises that follow,

387
00:12:48,880 --> 00:12:49,840
at least in this class,

388
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:51,820
we'll basically use the lowercase s throughout.

389
00:12:51,820 --> 00:12:53,720
Later on, when we write the operating system,

390
00:12:53,720 --> 00:12:55,540
we'll use files with the uppercase S,

391
00:12:55,540 --> 00:12:57,080
and you can compare them yourself.

392
00:12:57,080 --> 00:12:57,980
The main difference

393
00:12:57,980 --> 00:12:59,700
is really that with the uppercase S,

394
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:01,480
the file can actually contain some

395
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:03,040
preprocessing directives,

396
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:04,100
such as #include,

397
00:13:04,100 --> 00:13:05,560
#define, and so on.

398
00:13:05,560 --> 00:13:06,920
With the lowercase .s,

399
00:13:06,920 --> 00:13:08,180
all it contains is

400
00:13:08,180 --> 00:13:10,680
pure assembly instructions.

401
00:13:10,680 --> 00:13:11,560
That's how it is.

402
00:13:11,560 --> 00:13:13,620
This is something you can look into.

403
00:13:13,620 --> 00:13:16,720
So when we talk about a complete assembly program,

404
00:13:16,720 --> 00:13:17,600
that is, a complete

405
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:18,660
assembly program,

406
00:13:18,660 --> 00:13:19,660
which is a .s file,

407
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:21,820
what is it made up of?

408
00:13:21,820 --> 00:13:23,100
It is essentially made up of

409
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:24,900
statements one by one.

410
00:13:24,900 --> 00:13:25,720
Each statement,

411
00:13:25,720 --> 00:13:26,860
we call it a statement.

412
00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:28,360
In these slides,

413
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:29,620
I've also written the English terms.

414
00:13:29,620 --> 00:13:31,380
This is mainly to make it easier for you later,

415
00:13:31,380 --> 00:13:32,380
because a lot of material

416
00:13:32,380 --> 00:13:33,280
is still in English and you'll have to read it.

417
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:34,220
So when you see the English,

418
00:13:34,220 --> 00:13:34,880
if there are words

419
00:13:34,880 --> 00:13:36,320
whose exact meaning you're not sure of,

420
00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:37,340
you can also refer back to these.

421
00:13:37,340 --> 00:13:39,120
The things I've listed for you in the slides

422
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:41,160
Because basically,

423
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:42,980
the official documentation is all in English,

424
00:13:42,980 --> 00:13:44,880
so I hope everyone will get familiar with these technical terms

425
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:45,900
—I do want you to understand them.

426
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:47,900
If we only talk in Chinese,

427
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:49,280
it might cause some confusion.

428
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:50,360
Just want to mention that to you.

429
00:13:50,360 --> 00:13:53,940
So this is the statements, line by line.

430
00:13:53,940 --> 00:13:56,900
Now what format does each line of statement follow?

431
00:13:56,900 --> 00:13:57,860
The format is also very simple.

432
00:13:57,860 --> 00:13:59,860
Simple, it's just what I've listed for you here,

433
00:13:59,860 --> 00:14:01,940
which is to say it's actually made up of three parts,

434
00:14:01,940 --> 00:14:04,460
and the order is from left to right.

435
00:14:04,460 --> 00:14:05,500
The first part

436
00:14:05,500 --> 00:14:08,280
is something called a label.

437
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:10,400
A label,

438
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:12,140
which is what we'd call a tag or marker.

439
00:14:12,140 --> 00:14:13,320
Now after the label,

440
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:14,760
there must be a colon.

441
00:14:14,760 --> 00:14:16,960
So why put it in square brackets?

442
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:17,800
It means that

443
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:19,420
this item is optional

444
00:14:19,420 --> 00:14:19,860
and can be omitted.

445
00:14:19,860 --> 00:14:22,040
If all three items are in square brackets,

446
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:23,240
that means it supports line breaks,

447
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:23,700
right?

448
00:14:23,700 --> 00:14:25,140
That is to say, within this,

449
00:14:25,140 --> 00:14:26,500
we can support line breaks.

450
00:14:26,500 --> 00:14:29,000
Now, the second item,

451
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:29,680
called operation.

452
00:14:29,680 --> 00:14:30,580
Now, the operation,

453
00:14:30,580 --> 00:14:32,380
is relatively important.

454
00:14:32,380 --> 00:14:33,560
It is actually the real action.

455
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:34,520
It actually happens here,

456
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:35,720
the operation action.

457
00:14:35,720 --> 00:14:36,960
Now, this operation,

458
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:38,100
also has

459
00:14:38,100 --> 00:14:38,980
further subdivisions,

460
00:14:38,980 --> 00:14:39,780
which we'll look at in a moment.

461
00:14:39,780 --> 00:14:41,340
Now there's another part,

462
00:14:41,340 --> 00:14:42,720
which is the comment.

463
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:43,880
A comment is just a note or remark in the code,

464
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:44,440
right?

465
00:14:44,440 --> 00:14:46,500
This is something everyone should be familiar with.

466
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:48,160
So the basic syntax looks like this.

467
00:14:48,160 --> 00:14:49,220
And the square brackets,

468
00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:50,440
they indicate optional items.

469
00:14:50,440 --> 00:14:53,800
Now, in detail,

470
00:14:53,800 --> 00:14:55,000
let's take a look at this.

471
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:56,600
We mainly want to focus on

472
00:14:56,600 --> 00:14:57,060
the label,

473
00:14:57,060 --> 00:14:58,440
especially in the operation part,

474
00:14:58,440 --> 00:15:00,940
what kinds of variations there actually are.

475
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:03,540
So first, let's look at the label.

476
00:15:03,540 --> 00:15:04,560
As I just mentioned about the label,

477
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:05,040
that is to say,

478
00:15:05,040 --> 00:15:06,280
it is actually a marker or label,

479
00:15:06,280 --> 00:15:08,420
meaning that in this file,

480
00:15:08,420 --> 00:15:10,360
any identifier that ends with a colon

481
00:15:10,360 --> 00:15:11,660
it will be treated as a label

482
00:15:11,660 --> 00:15:13,660
As we go through this,

483
00:15:13,660 --> 00:15:14,820
let's look at the examples at the same time

484
00:15:14,820 --> 00:15:16,920
That might make a stronger impression.

485
00:15:16,920 --> 00:15:20,860
Let's pull up that...

486
00:15:20,860 --> 00:15:23,160
If the code is already on your machine,

487
00:15:23,160 --> 00:15:24,340
you can open it and browse through it yourself.

488
00:15:24,340 --> 00:15:25,900
That is to say, in our...

489
00:15:25,900 --> 00:15:28,820
in the code package we handed out to everyone,

490
00:15:28,820 --> 00:15:30,320
there is a directory called code,

491
00:15:30,320 --> 00:15:30,620
right?

492
00:15:30,620 --> 00:15:33,640
Under this code directory,

493
00:15:33,640 --> 00:15:34,740
there are two subdirectories.

494
00:15:34,740 --> 00:15:35,640
One is called ASM,

495
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:37,420
ASM is short for Assemble,

496
00:15:37,420 --> 00:15:38,120
meaning assembly.

497
00:15:38,120 --> 00:15:38,880
So for this part,

498
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:40,680
the examples for this course

499
00:15:40,680 --> 00:15:41,460
are all under this one.

500
00:15:41,460 --> 00:15:43,820
OS is for the operating system examples.

501
00:15:43,820 --> 00:15:45,620
Now, under Assemble,

502
00:15:45,620 --> 00:15:47,540
there are many small examples divided up here.

503
00:15:47,540 --> 00:15:49,580
So let's take a look.

504
00:15:49,580 --> 00:15:51,460
Right now we'll cover this most basic part,

505
00:15:51,460 --> 00:15:52,900
so let's look at this first example,

506
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:55,260
the example in this First directory.

507
00:15:55,260 --> 00:15:58,140
All of our files

508
00:15:58,140 --> 00:15:59,960
are called test.s,

509
00:15:59,960 --> 00:16:02,340
with a lowercase s as the extension.

510
00:16:02,340 --> 00:16:04,240
So you can open this file,

511
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:06,520
and this is our first

512
00:16:06,520 --> 00:16:08,740
RISC-V assembly example.

513
00:16:08,740 --> 00:16:09,520
In this example,

514
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:10,780
you can see here

515
00:16:10,780 --> 00:16:12,520
which parts are what we just talked about,

516
00:16:12,520 --> 00:16:13,620
the thing called a label.

517
00:16:13,620 --> 00:16:16,740
As we just discussed,

518
00:16:16,740 --> 00:16:17,560
this label must

519
00:16:17,560 --> 00:16:18,700
end with a colon, right?

520
00:16:18,700 --> 00:16:18,900
Isn't that so?

521
00:16:18,900 --> 00:16:20,040
So, this...

522
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:21,580
Like, over here is a label.

523
00:16:21,580 --> 00:16:23,440
And of course, on my side here,

524
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:24,700
I've added some syntax highlighting,

525
00:16:24,700 --> 00:16:26,240
so it might be a bit clearer to see.

526
00:16:26,240 --> 00:16:30,180
Now, this is also a label,

527
00:16:30,180 --> 00:16:31,500
and,

528
00:16:31,500 --> 00:16:32,160
one thing to notice,

529
00:16:32,160 --> 00:16:33,140
this label,

530
00:16:33,140 --> 00:16:34,440
it can actually be with

531
00:16:34,440 --> 00:16:36,160
other stuff,

532
00:16:36,160 --> 00:16:37,660
it can be written across two lines.

533
00:16:37,660 --> 00:16:39,900
Actually, if you write it like this,

534
00:16:39,900 --> 00:16:41,100
it's really equivalent to...

535
00:16:41,100 --> 00:16:43,760
I mean, these two lines,

536
00:16:43,760 --> 00:16:45,940
these two lines are really equivalent to one line.

537
00:16:45,940 --> 00:16:47,620
You get what I mean, right?

538
00:16:47,620 --> 00:16:48,740
Now, over here,

539
00:16:48,740 --> 00:16:51,140
I've written it strictly on one line.

540
00:16:51,140 --> 00:16:51,940
Please note that.

541
00:16:51,940 --> 00:16:54,720
It's one complete statement,

542
00:16:54,720 --> 00:16:57,080
that is, it fits on one line.

543
00:16:57,080 --> 00:16:59,680
So this,

544
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:02,640
what exactly is this label?

545
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:04,780
You can understand it this way:

546
00:17:04,780 --> 00:17:06,240
in assembly,

547
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:07,700
this label

548
00:17:07,700 --> 00:17:09,500
is essentially just an address.

549
00:17:09,500 --> 00:17:11,280
We give an address

550
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:12,080
a name,

551
00:17:12,080 --> 00:17:12,820
that's the idea behind it.

552
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:15,000
Because, as you all know,

553
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:16,880
this code,

554
00:17:16,880 --> 00:17:18,000
after it's finally compiled,

555
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:18,660
what does it become?

556
00:17:18,660 --> 00:17:20,100
After compilation,

557
00:17:20,100 --> 00:17:20,940
it's just machine instructions,

558
00:17:20,940 --> 00:17:21,740
right?

559
00:17:21,740 --> 00:17:22,780
So this kind of,

560
00:17:22,780 --> 00:17:24,820
this line here,

561
00:17:24,820 --> 00:17:26,520
In the end, it will actually be compiled into

562
00:17:26,520 --> 00:17:27,400
a single machine instruction.

563
00:17:27,400 --> 00:17:29,840
Now, as for a machine instruction,

564
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:30,220
as we know,

565
00:17:30,220 --> 00:17:33,040
the process of running a program on a machine,

566
00:17:33,040 --> 00:17:34,180
we already covered that

567
00:17:34,180 --> 00:17:35,680
in the first lecture.

568
00:17:35,680 --> 00:17:36,600
Let's review it.

569
00:17:36,600 --> 00:17:38,680
Basically, every machine instruction

570
00:17:38,680 --> 00:17:40,240
is loaded into memory,

571
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:41,540
and as it sits in memory,

572
00:17:41,540 --> 00:17:42,880
doesn't it have an address?

573
00:17:42,880 --> 00:17:45,760
Remember that animated demo of ours?

574
00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:48,860
A mini, that mini CPU,

575
00:17:48,860 --> 00:17:50,160
how does that mini CPU run?

576
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:51,600
Instructions are loaded in one by one

577
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:52,940
and executed, right?

578
00:17:52,940 --> 00:17:54,920
Every instruction has an address.

579
00:17:54,920 --> 00:17:56,580
Now, this stop label,

580
00:17:56,580 --> 00:17:57,380
you can actually think of it as

581
00:17:57,380 --> 00:17:59,480
It adds an address to this instruction,

582
00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:00,720
it attaches an address.

583
00:18:00,720 --> 00:18:01,720
So attaching it here,

584
00:18:01,720 --> 00:18:03,540
presumably means we'll later need to use this address

585
00:18:03,540 --> 00:18:04,380
to reference it.

586
00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:05,660
Some instructions

587
00:18:05,660 --> 00:18:07,160
don't have an address attached in front,

588
00:18:07,160 --> 00:18:08,060
which probably means we don't care

589
00:18:08,060 --> 00:18:08,900
about the address of that instruction.

590
00:18:08,900 --> 00:18:09,820
Now, suppose

591
00:18:09,820 --> 00:18:10,940
sometimes we do need to reference

592
00:18:10,940 --> 00:18:12,980
the address of this instruction,

593
00:18:12,980 --> 00:18:13,740
then we can

594
00:18:13,740 --> 00:18:15,180
put a label right here.

595
00:18:15,180 --> 00:18:17,660
Even for some variables we define,

596
00:18:17,660 --> 00:18:18,880
we can also add a label.

597
00:18:18,880 --> 00:18:20,780
Because everything in memory,

598
00:18:20,780 --> 00:18:21,540
like a global variable,

599
00:18:21,540 --> 00:18:22,440
may also have

600
00:18:22,440 --> 00:18:23,080
its own address.

601
00:18:23,080 --> 00:18:23,660
Think about it, isn't that so?

602
00:18:23,660 --> 00:18:24,860
So a label...

603
00:18:24,860 --> 00:18:25,900
in assembly,

604
00:18:25,900 --> 00:18:26,520
you can really just think of it as

605
00:18:26,520 --> 00:18:27,680
an address, and that's all there is to it.

606
00:18:27,680 --> 00:18:29,200
Alright.

607
00:18:29,200 --> 00:18:31,040
That's the concept of a label.

608
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:34,680
Very simple.

609
00:18:34,680 --> 00:18:37,140
Now moving further down,

610
00:18:37,140 --> 00:18:37,820
we have the operation.

611
00:18:37,820 --> 00:18:39,960
The form of an operation is a bit more complex,

612
00:18:39,960 --> 00:18:41,240
with quite a few variations.

613
00:18:41,240 --> 00:18:43,720
As for the operation,

614
00:18:43,720 --> 00:18:44,840
if we summarize it,

615
00:18:44,840 --> 00:18:46,320
it can take four forms.

616
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:48,100
The first one is the instruction.

617
00:18:48,100 --> 00:18:50,040
Because, as we said, the most basic thing is,

618
00:18:50,040 --> 00:18:50,860
in our assembly language,

619
00:18:50,860 --> 00:18:53,720
one statement actually corresponds to one machine instruction.

620
00:18:53,720 --> 00:18:56,320
So an instruction is called an instruction.

621
00:18:56,320 --> 00:18:59,240
a string that directly corresponds to binary machine instructions

622
00:18:59,240 --> 00:19:01,780
So in the example we just looked at,

623
00:19:01,780 --> 00:19:03,460
which ones are machine instructions?

624
00:19:03,460 --> 00:19:07,650
Take a look, everyone — which ones are machine instructions?

625
00:19:07,650 --> 00:19:10,330
Strictly speaking,

626
00:19:10,330 --> 00:19:11,690
all of these are actually machine instructions.

627
00:19:11,690 --> 00:19:13,310
But correspondingly, when we talk about

628
00:19:13,310 --> 00:19:14,970
the concept of an instruction,

629
00:19:14,970 --> 00:19:18,110
in these statements,

630
00:19:18,110 --> 00:19:19,530
this is the instruction.

631
00:19:19,530 --> 00:19:21,270
We'll learn later that

632
00:19:21,270 --> 00:19:23,630
the identifier add, this string,

633
00:19:23,630 --> 00:19:26,230
is a code name for a basic instruction in RISC-V,

634
00:19:26,230 --> 00:19:30,230
a basic instruction in RISC-V.

635
00:19:30,230 --> 00:19:31,650
This is what we call an instruction.

636
00:19:31,650 --> 00:19:33,430
Now some of you might ask,

637
00:19:33,430 --> 00:19:34,910
what about things like LRI here?

638
00:19:34,910 --> 00:19:35,890
Aren't those instructions too?

639
00:19:35,890 --> 00:19:37,510
They are instructions as well,

640
00:19:37,510 --> 00:19:38,330
but

641
00:19:38,330 --> 00:19:39,670
We'll talk about that later.

642
00:19:39,670 --> 00:19:40,470
This kind of instruction,

643
00:19:40,470 --> 00:19:42,210
we call it a pseudo-instruction.

644
00:19:42,210 --> 00:19:43,090
It still differs

645
00:19:43,090 --> 00:19:44,770
from the native instruction

646
00:19:44,770 --> 00:19:45,930
in some ways.

647
00:19:45,930 --> 00:19:47,670
Now, as for pseudo-instructions,

648
00:19:47,670 --> 00:19:49,510
on our course materials,

649
00:19:49,510 --> 00:19:50,270
it's written as,

650
00:19:50,270 --> 00:19:52,910
the English term is 'Pseudo Instruction'

651
00:19:52,910 --> 00:19:54,090
—meaning pseudo,

652
00:19:54,090 --> 00:19:54,950
a fake instruction.

653
00:19:54,950 --> 00:19:55,910
So what...

654
00:19:55,910 --> 00:19:57,390
why do we call it a fake instruction?

655
00:19:57,390 --> 00:19:57,750
The thing is,

656
00:19:57,750 --> 00:19:59,990
when you actually go look up

657
00:19:59,990 --> 00:20:02,130
the RISC-V manual,

658
00:20:02,130 --> 00:20:03,210
it actually...

659
00:20:03,210 --> 00:20:04,730
it doesn't correspond to a

660
00:20:04,730 --> 00:20:06,030
native

661
00:20:06,030 --> 00:20:08,930
This RISC-V instruction,

662
00:20:08,930 --> 00:20:10,190
rather, we say it

663
00:20:10,190 --> 00:20:12,510
exists purely from the perspective of writing code

664
00:20:12,510 --> 00:20:13,470
efficiently,

665
00:20:13,470 --> 00:20:14,350
purely for the convenience of

666
00:20:14,350 --> 00:20:15,830
our programmers when writing code.

667
00:20:15,830 --> 00:20:17,310
That is, it takes some

668
00:20:17,310 --> 00:20:20,010
hard-to-use instructions,

669
00:20:20,010 --> 00:20:20,890
basic instructions,

670
00:20:20,890 --> 00:20:22,790
or some variations of instructions,

671
00:20:22,790 --> 00:20:24,210
or some

672
00:20:24,210 --> 00:20:26,110
combinations of native instructions,

673
00:20:26,110 --> 00:20:28,470
and wraps them into a simple one,

674
00:20:28,470 --> 00:20:29,850
giving it an alias.

675
00:20:29,850 --> 00:20:31,290
You get what I mean here, right?

676
00:20:31,290 --> 00:20:32,310
It's given an alias,

677
00:20:32,310 --> 00:20:32,850
and then,

678
00:20:32,850 --> 00:20:34,130
to make it easier for us to use.

679
00:20:34,130 --> 00:20:36,950
So this pseudo-instruction,

680
00:20:36,950 --> 00:20:38,270
it's possible that a pseudo-instruction is

681
00:20:38,270 --> 00:20:38,810
Perhaps it's

682
00:20:38,810 --> 00:20:40,350
that is to say, as an assembler

683
00:20:40,350 --> 00:20:41,090
after we've written

684
00:20:41,090 --> 00:20:41,630
this assembly

685
00:20:41,630 --> 00:20:42,950
code,

686
00:20:42,950 --> 00:20:44,190
don't we hand it over to

687
00:20:44,190 --> 00:20:44,810
that Example

688
00:20:44,810 --> 00:20:45,850
assembler to

689
00:20:45,850 --> 00:20:48,350
process it?

690
00:20:48,350 --> 00:20:49,210
What does the assembler do?

691
00:20:49,210 --> 00:20:51,130
It takes this assembly language

692
00:20:51,130 --> 00:20:51,970
and translates it into what?

693
00:20:51,970 --> 00:20:52,990
Converts it into what?

694
00:20:52,990 --> 00:20:54,130
Doesn't it convert it into

695
00:20:54,130 --> 00:20:54,650
machine instructions?

696
00:20:54,650 --> 00:20:55,370
Think about it, isn't that right?

697
00:20:55,370 --> 00:20:57,550
Now, this assembly instruction,

698
00:20:57,550 --> 00:20:58,110
when it sees this kind of

699
00:20:58,110 --> 00:20:58,750
pseudo-instruction,

700
00:20:58,750 --> 00:21:00,250
it will break it down further.

701
00:21:00,250 --> 00:21:01,230
It is broken down into

702
00:21:01,230 --> 00:21:02,090
what we covered earlier—

703
00:21:02,090 --> 00:21:05,210
real instructions that are then processed.

704
00:21:05,210 --> 00:21:06,590
This is called a pseudo-instruction,

705
00:21:06,590 --> 00:21:09,930
and it is part of the assembly syntax as well.

706
00:21:09,930 --> 00:21:11,830
In our instruction set,

707
00:21:11,830 --> 00:21:12,930
you'll see that in the specification

708
00:21:12,930 --> 00:21:14,310
there is a definition for it too,

709
00:21:14,310 --> 00:21:18,510
but it is actually an alias for an instruction.

710
00:21:18,510 --> 00:21:19,390
You can think of it this way:

711
00:21:19,390 --> 00:21:21,770
a combination of instructions, or an alias for an instruction.

712
00:21:21,770 --> 00:21:25,850
As for what pseudo-instructions really are,

713
00:21:25,850 --> 00:21:27,630
we'll go over that gradually later on,

714
00:21:27,630 --> 00:21:28,690
and you can take your time to understand it then.

715
00:21:28,690 --> 00:21:32,590
So as for which pseudo-instructions exist,

716
00:21:32,590 --> 00:21:34,370
we'll naturally know once we've studied them.

717
00:21:34,370 --> 00:21:36,270
So here I'll just tell you directly:

718
00:21:36,270 --> 00:21:37,670
for example, this is a pseudo-instruction.

719
00:21:37,670 --> 00:21:40,070
It looks very similar to that instruction,

720
00:21:40,070 --> 00:21:44,770
but actually, after it is processed by the assembler,

721
00:21:44,770 --> 00:21:48,430
It actually gets translated into some other real instructions.

722
00:21:48,430 --> 00:21:50,270
These are called pseudo-instructions.

723
00:21:50,270 --> 00:21:53,550
Now, what else is there?

724
00:21:53,550 --> 00:21:56,670
There are also things called directives.

725
00:21:56,670 --> 00:21:58,650
You can look this up

726
00:21:58,650 --> 00:21:59,710
when you check the online documentation,

727
00:21:59,710 --> 00:22:00,410
it's all in there.

728
00:22:00,410 --> 00:22:01,550
So after I've talked about it,

729
00:22:01,550 --> 00:22:02,390
it will probably be more convenient

730
00:22:02,390 --> 00:22:03,330
for you to study it on your own.

731
00:22:03,330 --> 00:22:04,590
As for directives,

732
00:22:04,590 --> 00:22:04,990
they are called...

733
00:22:04,990 --> 00:22:06,590
In Chinese, we translate it

734
00:22:06,590 --> 00:22:07,950
as "指示" (zhǐshì), meaning instructions or directives.

735
00:22:07,950 --> 00:22:08,590
That's because the word 'directive'

736
00:22:08,590 --> 00:22:09,630
in its original meaning

737
00:22:09,630 --> 00:22:10,230
means instruction or directive.

738
00:22:10,230 --> 00:22:11,150
But if you feel

739
00:22:11,150 --> 00:22:11,990
this Chinese translation...

740
00:22:11,990 --> 00:22:13,030
because our Chinese translation...

741
00:22:13,030 --> 00:22:14,450
Actually, it's not official either.

742
00:22:14,450 --> 00:22:15,150
It's just my own

743
00:22:15,150 --> 00:22:16,510
referencing some things online,

744
00:22:16,510 --> 00:22:17,230
or my own understanding,

745
00:22:17,230 --> 00:22:17,710
that I wrote this with.

746
00:22:17,710 --> 00:22:18,330
So if you all

747
00:22:18,330 --> 00:22:19,390
if you feel this translation

748
00:22:19,390 --> 00:22:20,570
doesn't quite suit your taste,

749
00:22:20,570 --> 00:22:21,510
then just remember

750
00:22:21,510 --> 00:22:22,570
the original English text.

751
00:22:22,570 --> 00:22:23,590
Because memorizing the original

752
00:22:23,590 --> 00:22:24,310
is actually better.

753
00:22:24,310 --> 00:22:26,310
It might be more convenient.

754
00:22:26,310 --> 00:22:28,090
If you have a better understanding of the original text,

755
00:22:28,090 --> 00:22:30,670
you really don't have to force it into Chinese.

756
00:22:30,670 --> 00:22:32,290
Now, this fence instruction,

757
00:22:32,290 --> 00:22:33,710
we call it a fence operation,

758
00:22:33,710 --> 00:22:35,550
and some books also call it a fence operation.

759
00:22:35,550 --> 00:22:37,250
So this Chinese translation has quite a few variations.

760
00:22:37,250 --> 00:22:38,610
Please pay attention and distinguish between them.

761
00:22:38,610 --> 00:22:40,470
But in the original text, it's called a directive.

762
00:22:40,470 --> 00:22:42,030
What is a directive?

763
00:22:42,030 --> 00:22:43,490
What are its characteristics?

764
00:22:43,490 --> 00:22:45,050
Its characteristic is

765
00:22:45,050 --> 00:22:46,630
it must always start with a dot,

766
00:22:46,630 --> 00:22:48,850
something that begins with a small dot.

767
00:22:48,850 --> 00:22:49,770
Let's take a look

768
00:22:49,770 --> 00:22:51,210
at that example from just now,

769
00:22:51,210 --> 00:22:52,790
which ones are called directives.

770
00:22:52,790 --> 00:22:54,050
Everyone, take a look

771
00:22:54,050 --> 00:22:55,170
at which ones are directives.

772
00:22:55,170 --> 00:22:57,110
This one is a directive,

773
00:22:57,110 --> 00:22:57,450
right?

774
00:22:57,450 --> 00:22:59,490
This kind is also a directive.

775
00:22:59,490 --> 00:23:00,610
Do you all see it?

776
00:23:00,610 --> 00:23:02,830
These are all called directives.

777
00:23:02,830 --> 00:23:04,050
Over here, I've marked them out as well.

778
00:23:04,050 --> 00:23:05,230
This kind of directive,

779
00:23:05,230 --> 00:23:07,250
it actually does not belong

780
00:23:07,250 --> 00:23:09,730
to RISC-V instructions.

781
00:23:09,730 --> 00:23:12,290
It's actually part of the specification itself.

782
00:23:12,290 --> 00:23:13,250
It actually belongs to

783
00:23:13,250 --> 00:23:16,510
syntax that the assembler defines itself.

784
00:23:16,510 --> 00:23:17,970
Syntax it defines on its own.

785
00:23:17,970 --> 00:23:20,070
It's really meant for the assembler to read.

786
00:23:20,070 --> 00:23:21,350
Based on your

787
00:23:21,350 --> 00:23:23,490
directives like this,

788
00:23:23,490 --> 00:23:25,990
the assembler will actually do some special processing.

789
00:23:25,990 --> 00:23:27,410
It will perform some special processing.

790
00:23:27,410 --> 00:23:29,210
It's a bit like preprocessing,

791
00:23:29,210 --> 00:23:32,310
things like include, or define, that kind of stuff.

792
00:23:32,310 --> 00:23:33,950
But I'd say they're not exactly the same.

793
00:23:33,950 --> 00:23:36,990
But it's something used by the assembler.

794
00:23:36,990 --> 00:23:40,970
What exactly do these things represent?

795
00:23:40,970 --> 00:23:43,590
We'll take a look at them when we come across them later.

796
00:23:43,590 --> 00:23:45,030
For now, we're just going over

797
00:23:45,030 --> 00:23:46,290
some general concepts here.

798
00:23:46,290 --> 00:23:48,170
Like this .and thing,

799
00:23:48,170 --> 00:23:50,150
you can probably guess what this kind of thing is for, right?

800
00:23:50,150 --> 00:23:50,970
What does .and mean?

801
00:23:50,970 --> 00:23:52,750
That is, telling the assembler

802
00:23:52,750 --> 00:23:54,230
that our program here

803
00:23:54,230 --> 00:23:55,310
is done.

804
00:23:55,310 --> 00:23:55,670
Still with me?

805
00:23:55,670 --> 00:23:57,350
If you put this .end after it

806
00:23:57,350 --> 00:23:58,450
and then write some more stuff,

807
00:23:58,450 --> 00:23:59,630
you can try it out later—

808
00:23:59,630 --> 00:24:00,690
it really won't get assembled;

809
00:24:00,690 --> 00:24:02,330
it simply won't be processed at all.

810
00:24:02,330 --> 00:24:05,090
Now, this macro thing here—

811
00:24:05,090 --> 00:24:06,530
can you tell what this is?

812
00:24:06,530 --> 00:24:07,910
What is it defining?

813
00:24:07,910 --> 00:24:08,870
It's defining a macro.

814
00:24:08,870 --> 00:24:09,490
Right?

815
00:24:09,490 --> 00:24:10,230
A macro.

816
00:24:10,230 --> 00:24:11,350
This .endm here

817
00:24:11,350 --> 00:24:11,810
simply marks

818
00:24:11,810 --> 00:24:14,290
that everything between this macro and this .endm

819
00:24:14,290 --> 00:24:15,590
is actually defining the macro.

820
00:24:15,590 --> 00:24:16,330
So what is the macro?

821
00:24:16,330 --> 00:24:17,490
We'll see it again in a moment.

822
00:24:17,490 --> 00:24:19,250
The .text part,

823
00:24:19,250 --> 00:24:21,230
is really just telling everyone that

824
00:24:21,230 --> 00:24:23,310
the instructions we generate,

825
00:24:23,310 --> 00:24:24,890
need to be placed into that thing called the

826
00:24:24,890 --> 00:24:27,070
.text section.

827
00:24:27,070 --> 00:24:28,890
You still remember this concept, right?

828
00:24:28,890 --> 00:24:30,390
Last time, why did we

829
00:24:30,390 --> 00:24:32,310
go on and on about those

830
00:24:32,310 --> 00:24:34,330
compilation and linking concepts, the ELF stuff?

831
00:24:34,330 --> 00:24:36,130
Because we actually use all of that right here.

832
00:24:36,130 --> 00:24:37,750
So when you see these things,

833
00:24:37,750 --> 00:24:39,730
think back to what we covered earlier.

834
00:24:39,730 --> 00:24:40,450
Knowledge is

835
00:24:40,450 --> 00:24:43,330
always built up — the earlier stuff lays the groundwork for what comes later.

836
00:24:43,330 --> 00:24:45,190
As for .global,

837
00:24:45,190 --> 00:24:46,890
it's a bit like the thing in C,

838
00:24:46,890 --> 00:24:48,590
like when you define a global variable.

839
00:24:48,590 --> 00:24:50,550
It's telling you that this thing here,

840
00:24:50,550 --> 00:24:52,630
this identifier,

841
00:24:52,630 --> 00:24:53,710
Now, this 'start' thing,

842
00:24:53,710 --> 00:24:55,370
it's a global thing.

843
00:24:55,370 --> 00:24:57,010
So what exactly is this 'start'?

844
00:24:57,010 --> 00:24:59,150
It's yet another address.

845
00:24:59,150 --> 00:25:00,570
You can think of this address as

846
00:25:00,570 --> 00:25:02,530
just the name of a function.

847
00:25:02,530 --> 00:25:03,470
You see what I mean, right?

848
00:25:03,470 --> 00:25:04,990
You can understand it that way.

849
00:25:04,990 --> 00:25:07,310
Because this function 'start' here,

850
00:25:07,310 --> 00:25:08,970
it's visible externally,

851
00:25:08,970 --> 00:25:10,830
so they just use it like this,

852
00:25:10,830 --> 00:25:12,250
somewhat like what you'd define in C,

853
00:25:12,250 --> 00:25:13,210
the concept of a global variable.

854
00:25:13,210 --> 00:25:14,430
extern

855
00:25:14,430 --> 00:25:15,750
It's a bit like that extern,

856
00:25:15,750 --> 00:25:16,910
a bit like that extern,

857
00:25:16,910 --> 00:25:19,510
but of course you can't compare them completely.

858
00:25:19,510 --> 00:25:20,470
Can't compare them, yeah.

859
00:25:20,550 --> 00:25:24,910
This is the third thing we've talked about, right?

860
00:25:24,910 --> 00:25:26,150
pseudo-op

861
00:25:26,150 --> 00:25:27,750
This is called a pseudo-instruction

862
00:25:27,750 --> 00:25:29,030
Please pay attention to this kind of pseudo-instruction

863
00:25:29,030 --> 00:25:30,670
It does not correspond to an actual instruction

864
00:25:30,670 --> 00:25:32,130
It only exists in the assembler

865
00:25:32,130 --> 00:25:33,810
If you go look at the RISC-V spec

866
00:25:33,810 --> 00:25:36,110
or in Intel's instruction manual

867
00:25:36,110 --> 00:25:37,170
you won't find these things there

868
00:25:37,170 --> 00:25:40,630
You can only find it in the GNU Assembler's manual

869
00:25:40,630 --> 00:25:42,790
You'll see its definition in the assembler manual

870
00:25:42,790 --> 00:25:44,090
So please note this

871
00:25:44,090 --> 00:25:46,150
And then, there's more

872
00:25:46,150 --> 00:25:47,910
That is, in the Operation field

873
00:25:47,910 --> 00:25:48,790
what else can appear?

874
00:25:48,790 --> 00:25:50,750
You can also have what is called a macro

875
00:25:50,750 --> 00:25:51,650
This is a macro

876
00:25:51,650 --> 00:25:53,170
What is a macro?

877
00:25:53,170 --> 00:25:56,210
It's actually quite similar to the concept of macros defined in C

878
00:25:56,210 --> 00:25:58,290
As we know, in C we can define a macro

879
00:25:58,290 --> 00:26:00,030
and then in the C code

880
00:26:00,030 --> 00:26:01,650
use the macro to substitute for it

881
00:26:01,650 --> 00:26:02,190
Right?

882
00:26:02,190 --> 00:26:04,290
This concept is pretty simple, isn't it?

883
00:26:04,290 --> 00:26:05,070
Look here.

884
00:26:05,070 --> 00:26:06,390
We defined a macro.

885
00:26:06,390 --> 00:26:07,070
do nothing

886
00:26:07,070 --> 00:26:08,230
Actually, I didn't do anything here.

887
00:26:08,230 --> 00:26:09,950
The name of this macro already tells us

888
00:26:09,950 --> 00:26:11,110
that this section below

889
00:26:11,110 --> 00:26:12,610
these two instructions really do nothing.

890
00:26:12,610 --> 00:26:14,910
Because this nop, we'll learn about it later,

891
00:26:14,910 --> 00:26:16,510
is actually an empty instruction.

892
00:26:16,510 --> 00:26:17,790
It doesn't actually do anything.

893
00:26:17,790 --> 00:26:19,850
We'll talk about it when we get there.

894
00:26:19,850 --> 00:26:22,150
Anyway, the point is, later on,

895
00:26:22,150 --> 00:26:23,270
look here,

896
00:26:23,270 --> 00:26:25,870
we invoked the macro do nothing here.

897
00:26:25,870 --> 00:26:29,850
Now, when the assembler processes this piece of code,

898
00:26:29,850 --> 00:26:31,150
it will take the macro do nothing

899
00:26:31,150 --> 00:26:32,210
and expand it.

900
00:26:32,210 --> 00:26:34,390
This is the same concept as preprocessing in C.

901
00:26:34,390 --> 00:26:35,230
After expansion,

902
00:26:35,230 --> 00:26:38,970
as you can see, this becomes two nop instructions,

903
00:26:38,970 --> 00:26:41,230
and nop itself is again a microinstruction.

904
00:26:41,230 --> 00:26:44,290
So microinstructions are then translated into machine instructions.

905
00:26:44,290 --> 00:26:45,670
We'll take another look at that later on.

906
00:26:45,670 --> 00:26:47,690
So it has this kind of macro substitution,

907
00:26:47,690 --> 00:26:48,790
this kind of effect.

908
00:26:48,790 --> 00:26:51,690
This is what happens when a macro is called,

909
00:26:51,690 --> 00:26:53,130
that is, this again.

910
00:26:53,130 --> 00:26:55,570
All right, that's...

911
00:26:55,570 --> 00:26:58,190
So basically,

912
00:26:58,190 --> 00:26:59,550
with regard to the .option directive,

913
00:26:59,550 --> 00:27:00,750
the things involved here,

914
00:27:00,750 --> 00:27:01,610
we've covered for you.

915
00:27:01,610 --> 00:27:04,470
Of course, there are still some more complicated parts,

916
00:27:04,470 --> 00:27:05,190
which we won't go into.

917
00:27:05,190 --> 00:27:06,390
Basically, we've just

918
00:27:06,390 --> 00:27:07,630
touched on the basic ones we'll be using,

919
00:27:07,630 --> 00:27:08,870
here for everyone.

920
00:27:08,870 --> 00:27:10,310
Now, the last part

921
00:27:10,310 --> 00:27:11,570
What we mean is comments.

922
00:27:11,570 --> 00:27:12,970
As for comments,

923
00:27:12,970 --> 00:27:15,690
there are actually many ways to write them.

924
00:27:15,690 --> 00:27:17,490
Including the kind that looks like C comments,

925
00:27:17,490 --> 00:27:18,290
those are supported too.

926
00:27:18,290 --> 00:27:21,010
But in assembly language,

927
00:27:21,010 --> 00:27:22,210
the most commonly used form is one

928
00:27:22,210 --> 00:27:23,990
that starts with a sharp sign.

929
00:27:23,990 --> 00:27:24,870
Please keep that in mind.

930
00:27:24,870 --> 00:27:25,550
I mentioned this before,

931
00:27:25,550 --> 00:27:26,470
it's just the common convention.

932
00:27:26,470 --> 00:27:27,030
There are others.

933
00:27:27,030 --> 00:27:29,170
If you see other forms later on,

934
00:27:29,170 --> 00:27:30,770
like using a semicolon,

935
00:27:30,770 --> 00:27:32,230
or double slashes,

936
00:27:32,230 --> 00:27:33,710
or something like that,

937
00:27:33,710 --> 00:27:35,410
don't say the professor never taught it.

938
00:27:35,410 --> 00:27:36,030
This is just because

939
00:27:36,030 --> 00:27:37,070
on our side,

940
00:27:37,070 --> 00:27:38,250
for simplicity,

941
00:27:38,250 --> 00:27:40,010
So we've listed for you

942
00:27:40,010 --> 00:27:40,990
the most commonly used way,

943
00:27:40,990 --> 00:27:42,430
which is to start with the hash symbol.

944
00:27:42,430 --> 00:27:43,990
When you start with the hash symbol,

945
00:27:43,990 --> 00:27:45,410
in C it doesn't mean a comment,

946
00:27:45,410 --> 00:27:46,750
it's a preprocessor directive.

947
00:27:46,750 --> 00:27:48,990
But in assembly,

948
00:27:48,990 --> 00:27:49,790
this is a comment.

949
00:27:49,790 --> 00:27:51,630
See, here it starts with the hash symbol,

950
00:27:51,630 --> 00:27:52,770
and over here they all start with the hash symbol.

951
00:27:52,770 --> 00:27:54,650
That is to say, when it's being processed,

952
00:27:54,650 --> 00:27:56,350
starting from this hash symbol,

953
00:27:56,350 --> 00:27:58,310
this line is processed line by line,

954
00:27:58,310 --> 00:28:00,390
and everything from this hash symbol onward

955
00:28:00,390 --> 00:28:02,530
is considered a comment.

956
00:28:02,530 --> 00:28:03,590
So it's the same here.

957
00:28:03,590 --> 00:28:04,950
For this line,

958
00:28:04,950 --> 00:28:07,490
the beginning is still some normal operations,

959
00:28:07,490 --> 00:28:09,290
but from here on out,

960
00:28:09,290 --> 00:28:11,990
the color all turns into this purple color.

961
00:28:11,990 --> 00:28:12,270
Right?

962
00:28:12,270 --> 00:28:14,090
It's all covered now.

963
00:28:14,090 --> 00:28:16,810
So basically, that means

964
00:28:16,810 --> 00:28:17,490
once you understand this,

965
00:28:17,490 --> 00:28:18,790
you can basically write your own

966
00:28:18,790 --> 00:28:21,750
very simple assembly file

967
00:28:21,750 --> 00:28:22,330
quite easily, right?

968
00:28:22,330 --> 00:28:22,990
Take a look.

969
00:28:22,990 --> 00:28:28,140
So, about assembly,

970
00:28:28,140 --> 00:28:30,100
this basic, super-basic

971
00:28:30,100 --> 00:28:33,740
what do you call it,

972
00:28:33,740 --> 00:28:34,660
syntax stuff,

973
00:28:34,660 --> 00:28:36,120
we'll stop our introduction here.
